		{"id":"424699b5822ce557","type":"group","x":2480,"y":-1080,"width":300,"height":320,"label":"Indirect Pathway"},
		{"id":"18c7cf4821d62f68","type":"group","x":3920,"y":-1450,"width":2240,"height":740,"label":"Neurons in the Striatum"},
		{"id":"8e2a9700b85d38b3","type":"group","x":1955,"y":-1080,"width":290,"height":533,"label":"Direct Pathway"},
		{"id":"68bed711364a5ba1","type":"group","x":4300,"y":-1142,"width":620,"height":145,"label":"The Striatum"},
		{"id":"fc3df42fe7a1a8f2","type":"text","text":"# Cortex","x":1975,"y":-627,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3b6ef2dc344a4155","type":"text","text":"#### Thalamus","x":1975,"y":-840,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d8b7c4da8227d70b","type":"text","text":"#### GPi / SNr","x":2040,"y":-1060,"width":120,"height":60},
		{"id":"280b102cc446d31c","type":"text","text":"Hyperdirect Pathway","x":3480,"y":-1355,"width":190,"height":60},
		{"id":"2e76e4bc4d3eadeb","type":"text","text":"Emotion/Motivation Areas","x":2227,"y":1239,"width":289,"height":60},
		{"id":"25faca9acff7e7b0","type":"text","text":"VApc, VAmc, VLcr, MDpl","x":1871,"y":1028,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"abca451e2d1cdd97","type":"text","text":"VAmc, VLm, MD","x":2246,"y":1027,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a11b9a592b6346c5","type":"text","text":"SNr / GPi","x":2246,"y":819,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cbbe8d23a5cbb721","type":"text","text":"SNr / GPi","x":1871,"y":820,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"31db6ec53e169af2","type":"text","text":"# Striatum\n- Inhibitory via Interneurons\n$$ Straitum \\xrightarrow{ACh} \\text{ }\\xrightarrow{GABA} Net = Inhibition  $$","x":2160,"y":-1411,"width":415,"height":171},
		{"id":"1cc8d81f6f2043ac","type":"text","text":"Emotion/Motivation Areas","x":2227,"y":420,"width":289,"height":60},
		{"id":"918aef6cade15076","type":"text","text":"Because axons of cortical neurons terminate on a far smaller number of striatal neurons, there is considerable convergence of cortical information in the striatum.","x":2139,"y":-140,"width":421,"height":180},
		{"id":"5c0592e70fb641dd","type":"text","text":"Caudate Nucleus","x":1872,"y":620,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8554d63c510543d9","type":"text","text":"Ventral Striatum","x":2246,"y":620,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"dd542f594e7d1bbd","type":"text","text":"SNc","x":3180,"y":-1355,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"ef0324198a502223","type":"text","text":"(ACA, anterior cingulate area; CMA,\ncingulate motor area; DLPFC, dorsolateral prefrontal cortex;\nFEF, frontal eye field; GPi, internal segment of the globus\npallidus; LOFC, lateral orbitofrontal cortex; M1, primary motor\ncortex; MDpl, mediodorsal nucleus of thalamus, lateral part;\nMOFC, medial orbitofrontal cortex; PMC, premotor cortex;\nSEF, supplementary eye field; SMA, supplementary motor\narea; SNr, substantia nigra pars reticulata; VAmc, ventral\nanterior nucleus of thalamus, magnocellular part; VApc, ventral\nanterior nucleus of thalamus, parvocellular part; VLcr, ventro-\nlateral nucleus of thalamus, caudal part, rostral division; VLm,\nventrolateral nucleus of thalamus, medial part; VLo, ventrola-\nteral nucleus of thalamus, pars oralis.) ","x":2640,"y":481,"width":376,"height":605},
		{"id":"0b8cc87718e46a31","type":"text","text":"#### Dopaminergic Systems\n\nThe sources of this information have not been identified, but they may include - \n- Subcortical areas such as the superior colliculus, \n- the pedunculopontine nucleus, \n- the raphe nuclei, the lateral habenular nucleus, the amygdala, or limbic areas of cortex","x":2727,"y":1628,"width":393,"height":292},
		{"id":"4a83f6f95f160068","type":"text","text":"Cholinergic interneurons in the striatum are also activated in rewarded behavioral tasks.","x":3240,"y":1408,"width":340,"height":105},
		{"id":"db4c485f8dd52498","type":"text","text":"## The Basal Ganglia in Behavior \n\n","x":2727,"y":1408,"width":393,"height":105},
		{"id":"930f6e0b46dec2d6","type":"text","text":"### Areas per function:\n##### 1. Motor\n- M1, primary motor cortex\n- PM, premotor cortex\n- SMA, supplementary motor area\n##### 2. Oculomotor Areas\n- FEF, frontal eye field\n- SEF supplementary eye field\n#### 3. Executive/Associative\n- DLPFC, dorsolateral prefrontal cortex\n- LOFC, lateral orbitofrontal cortex\n#### 4. Emotion/Motivation\n- ACA, anterior cingulate area\n- MOFC, medial orbitofrontal cortex","x":3140,"y":477,"width":445,"height":613},
		{"id":"851213879548bfa2","type":"text","text":"- [ ] Replicate","x":4315,"y":1179,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"726d25db096eb746","type":"text","text":"![[image-33.png]]","x":3840,"y":370,"width":1200,"height":630},
		{"id":"0f2c0497e19f2c08","type":"text","text":"Note: Dopamine is NOT the molecule of Pleasure it is the molecule of Novelty","x":3254,"y":1728,"width":312,"height":93},
		{"id":"d44a6ef9cd6f384b","type":"text","text":"Their discharge is briefly reduced in\nresponse to rewards, reinforcements, noxious stimuli,\nand other behaviorally salient stimuli.","x":3720,"y":1383,"width":340,"height":155},
		{"id":"b805bcd41037f637","type":"text","text":"How does the putamen circuit function to help execute patterns of movement? \nLittle is known about this function. \n**However, when a portion of the circuit is damaged or blocked, certain patterns of movement become severely abnormal.** \n\n- Lesions in the globus pallidus frequently lead to spontaneous and often continuous writhing movements of a hand, an arm, the neck, or the face. These movements are called athetosis.\n- Lesion in the subthalamus often leads to sudden flailing movements of an entire limb, a condition called hemiballismus.\n- Multiple small lesions in the putamen lead to flicking movements in the hands, face, and other parts of the body called chorea.\n- Lesions of the substantia nigra -> [[Parkinson’s disease]]","x":3920,"y":-100,"width":630,"height":380},
		{"id":"4a2c5b1ae01d76e0","type":"text","text":"Add to CNS Pathology? \n- Abnormal Function in the Putamen Circuit: Athetosis, Hemiballismus, and Chorea. \n[Guyton p.721]","x":4037,"y":-400,"width":396,"height":189,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"00d825b344f16564","type":"text","text":"D1 Receptors","x":3960,"y":-1172,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"4cfe836fb3c49537","type":"text","text":"# OUTPUT","x":1975,"y":-460,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"18067ca618e59445","type":"text","text":"D2 Reecptors","x":2760,"y":-1355,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4a63892247c2c3b4","type":"text","text":"#### Subthalamic Nucleus","x":2494,"y":-836,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"09ad97ba6f0d8da8","type":"text","text":"#### GPe","x":2575,"y":-1060,"width":88,"height":60},
		{"id":"38474b27f4bcf2ba","type":"text","text":"Similarly, the number of neurons in the pallidum and\nsubstantia nigra is smaller than that in the striatum,\nallowing further convergence along the direct and\nindirect pathways. However, given the somatotopic\narrangement of striatopallidal and pallido-subthalamic\nprojections, it appears that convergence occurs largely\nwithin rather than between the different basal ganglia–\nthalamocortical circuits and subcircuits","x":2660,"y":-226,"width":461,"height":353,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"09b093f9ca690429","type":"text","text":"But if pallidal activation during movement is insignificant,  (acc to point 2), why would the neuron topography matter? (point 1)\n\nThough this doesn't directly prove the focus-model to be correct","x":2860,"y":-2410,"width":340,"height":211,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"7f974ffc7346ce60","type":"text","text":"# Flaws\n- The hypothesis would require that axons in the indirect and hyperdirect pathways diffusely target large areas of the pallidum in order to prevent unwanted movements, whereas the direct pathway would act on small areas of the internal pallidal segment to selectively facilitate the selected movement. Neither of these anatomical prerequisites is supported by anatomic studies, which indicate instead that inputs of the indirect pathway to the internal pallidal segment from the subthalamic nucleus are highly topographic. \n- Furthermore, pallidal activation during movement initiation is generally considered to occur too late to play a significant role in the selection of movement.\n\n","x":2234,"y":-2520,"width":512,"height":430},
		{"id":"bc9b19b37f742d3b","type":"text","text":"SNr / GPi","x":1500,"y":818,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"64a9b4063aa51768","type":"text","text":"SNr / GPi","x":1125,"y":819,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"865a7d99f52dbeb3","type":"text","text":"VLo, VLm, VApc ","x":1125,"y":1027,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"98ad44a0795e6ede","type":"text","text":"SNc","x":1300,"y":-1355,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"915001f0179eaf69","type":"text","text":"Executive/Associative Areas","x":1852,"y":1240,"width":289,"height":60},
		{"id":"b4a253dd2bfc5891","type":"text","text":"MDpl, VLcr, VApc","x":1500,"y":1026,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7d3e5e71716e5f86","type":"text","text":"Changes in neural activity in a variety of stimulus-triggered movement tasks occur well after movement-related activities in the cerebral cortex or cerebellum, indicating that the\n### Basal ganglia do not participate in the initiation of such movements. ","x":1062,"y":1620,"width":376,"height":280},
		{"id":"9c83f758571f0c36","type":"text","text":"Motor circuit has a role in the **preparation of movement**","x":1062,"y":1480,"width":376,"height":80},
		{"id":"8f74f9a5b9028275","type":"text","text":"# BASAL NUCLEI","x":320,"y":-1112,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"74bedac8fbd2207d","type":"text","text":"Caudate Nucleus","x":1500,"y":619,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5e75515a36125b5d","type":"text","text":"Putamen","x":1126,"y":619,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6066512ea9d459be","type":"text","text":"## Functions","x":-312,"y":-780,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"16dcd2309b635f55","type":"text","text":"Executive/Associative Areas","x":1852,"y":421,"width":289,"height":60},
		{"id":"c1af38d47dc37b06","type":"text","text":"# Cortex","x":2240,"y":-1660,"width":256,"height":60},
		{"id":"1f21e639e7623308","type":"text","text":"D1 Receptors","x":1730,"y":-1355,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"43c39024dfbd9de6","type":"text","text":"Oculomotor Areas","x":1485,"y":420,"width":280,"height":60},
		{"id":"bbe8209d15965cdb","type":"text","text":"Ventral Pallidum (output nucleus of limbic loop)","x":-422,"y":-2794,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a3d0501e4e064bc5","type":"text","text":"Globus Pallidus externus","x":-1137,"y":-3150,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c82f5aff234058bd","type":"text","text":"Substantia nigra pars reticularis","x":180,"y":-2824,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6440b6ec724a6a15","type":"text","text":"Substantia nigra pars compacta","x":540,"y":-2824,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5d93ce3ceb5d3dcc","type":"text","text":"[[Substantia nigra]]","x":360,"y":-2564,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e60e281e6a5a0988","type":"text","text":"Subthalamic Nucleus\n","x":-80,"y":-2564,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7d9155a6926a2433","type":"text","text":"The finding that individual neurons in the basal ganglia tend to be concerned with either the preparation or the execution of motor action \n#### suggests that these functions are mediated by separate subcircuits in the motor circuit","x":1397,"y":2120,"width":402,"height":260},
		{"id":"83e7fec759785261","type":"text","text":"Oculomotor Areas","x":1485,"y":1238,"width":280,"height":60},
		{"id":"791b4d3128aac372","type":"text","text":"Motor Areas","x":1103,"y":1239,"width":294,"height":59},
		{"id":"f17223181f43086f","type":"text","text":"Dorsal Pallidum","x":-960,"y":-2794,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f282966b75400904","type":"text","text":"Globus Pallidus internus","x":-780,"y":-3150,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7613546dc725d669","type":"text","text":"Pallidum","x":-672,"y":-2564,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"351b38c50a62546a","type":"text","text":"## Components","x":-672,"y":-2001,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6bdef8ed4a6dbc15","type":"text","text":"Motor Areas","x":1103,"y":421,"width":294,"height":59},
		{"id":"3bd70a3c1ca5c76e","type":"text","text":"Respective Territories","x":720,"y":820,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"#878787"},
		{"id":"7e58dd971bdcde70","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20251115120213.png","x":101,"y":-200,"width":689,"height":525},
		{"id":"3efbbe9b15e76303","type":"text","text":"![[image-32.png]] \n- [ ] Replicate for anatomical understanding","x":-1317,"y":1320,"width":800,"height":540},
		{"id":"128aea88b841f7a0","type":"text","text":"What I used to think for the longest time: 1 circuit from PFC to Motor Cortex","x":1218,"y":-139,"width":542,"height":59},
		{"id":"ab217da6fa50d9a8","type":"text","text":"## **A. Motor Functions (Primary Domain)**\n\n1. **Initiation of voluntary movements**\n    \n2. **Scaling of movement amplitude and velocity**\n    \n3. **Selection of desired motor programs**\n    \n4. **Suppression of competing / unwanted motor programs**\n    \n5. **Automation of learned motor sequences (habitual motor learning)**\n    \n6. **Regulation of muscle tone (indirect pathway → inhibits excess tone)**\n    \n7. **Smooth execution of movement (D1 direct / D2 indirect balance)**\n    \n8. **Motor learning via reinforcement (dopamine-dependent plasticity)**\n    \n9. **Postural set maintenance (via brainstem projections)**\n    \n10. **Stopping movements (hyperdirect pathway from cortex → STN → GPi → braking signal)**\n    \n\nThese functions correspond to the **motor loop**:  \nCortex (SMA / PMC) → Putamen → GPi/SNr → VL Thalamus → Cortex\n\n---\n\n## **B. Oculomotor Functions**\n\n1. **Initiation of saccades** (particularly express saccades)\n    \n2. **Selection of saccadic targets**\n    \n3. **Suppression of unwanted eye movements**\n    \n4. **Control of gaze shifts and fixation**\n    \n5. **Sequencing saccadic eye movement patterns**\n    \n\nCorresponds to the **oculomotor loop**:  \nFrontal Eye Fields → Caudate (body) → SNr → SC → FEF\n\n---\n\n## **C. Cognitive / Executive Functions (Associative Loop)**\n\n1. **Action selection in cognitive tasks** (rule-based decisions)\n    \n2. **Working memory gating**\n    \n3. **Planning and sequencing of complex behavior**\n    \n4. **Attention shifting and cognitive flexibility**\n    \n5. **Learning stimulus–response associations**\n    \n6. **Habit formation (cognitive habits)**\n    \n7. **Evaluating predicted outcomes**\n    \n8. **Gating of prefrontal cortical activity** (decides what info enters PFC)\n    \n9. **Procedural learning** beyond motor domain\n    \n\nCorresponds to the **associative loop**:  \nDLPFC → Caudate (head) → GPi/SNr → MD Thalamus → DLPFC\n\n---\n\n## **D. Limbic / Emotional Functions (Limbic Loop)**\n\n1. **Motivated behavior control**\n    \n2. **Reward-based decision making**\n    \n3. **Action bias: approach vs avoidance**\n    \n4. **Emotional action selection (freeze, explore, withdraw, approach)**\n    \n5. **Reinforcement learning (via dopamine prediction errors)**\n    \n6. **Assigning motivational value to stimuli**\n    \n7. **Addiction and compulsive behaviour circuits (ventral striatum)**\n    \n8. **Gating emotional behaviours via ventral GP → MD thalamus → OFC/ACC**\n    \n9. **Integration of autonomic/motor responses with emotional state**\n    \n\nCorresponds to the **limbic loop**:  \nAmygdala/OFC/ACC → Nucleus Accumbens (ventral striatum) → Ventral Pallidum → MD Thalamus → ACC/OFC\n\n---\n\n# **E. Brainstem and Autonomic Modulation**\n\nVia projections to:\n\n- **Pedunculopontine nucleus (PPN)**\n    \n- **Reticulospinal systems**\n    \n- **Superior colliculus**\n    \n- **Midbrain locomotor region**\n    \n\nFunctions include:\n\n1. **Locomotor initiation**\n    \n2. **Startle modulation**\n    \n3. **Muscle tone via reticulospinal control**\n    \n4. **Integration of autonomic elements in movement**\n    \n5. **Pattern selection for stereotyped movements (e.g., gait)**\n    \n\n---\n\n# **F. Inhibitory “Gating” as the Core Function**\n\nAll basal ganglia functions fundamentally reduce to:  \n**Gating (disinhibition) of thalamocortical and brainstem motor/oculomotor/emotional channels.**\n\nThe BG does not directly command muscles or eyes — it **releases (disinhibits)** selected circuits and **suppresses** all others.\n\n---","x":-2800,"y":-953,"width":838,"height":2121},
		{"id":"2c585da1e93cfbd8","type":"text","text":"1. Planning and execution of voluntary movements\n2. Regulation of subconscious movements\n3. Control of muscle tone\n4. Control of reflex motor activity\n5. Control of automatic associated movements\n6. Role in arousal mechanism","x":-2623,"y":-1200,"width":483,"height":194},
		{"id":"808a7ed191a93629","type":"text","text":"Putamen","x":-1892,"y":-2794,"width":250,"height":60},
