		{"id":"78f4dad61c8f8072","type":"text","text":"Nucleus Accembens","x":-2460,"y":-3020,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b84c8d878b1c7840","type":"text","text":"Olefactory Tubercle","x":-2100,"y":-3020,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d4069ea7bf78673e","type":"text","text":"Lentiform Nucleus","x":-1582,"y":-3360,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0952f9e33b8430a3","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20251117160008.png","x":-1317,"y":-49,"width":800,"height":1247},
		{"id":"636d48fe74f6f62a","type":"text","text":"4 different parallel loops","x":1365,"y":33,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ee9e8f39dd08bba6","type":"text","text":"One especially important cortical area is the posterior parietal cortex, which is the locus of the spatial coordinates for motor control of all parts of the body, as well as for the relationship of the body and its parts to all its surroundings. \n\nLesions of the posterior parietal cortex produce an inability to perceive objects accurately through normally functioning sensory mechanisms, a condition called agnosia.","x":5260,"y":370,"width":445,"height":270,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"f071e1c262587938","type":"text","text":"# D2 Medium Spiny Neuron","x":4640,"y":-1117,"width":250,"height":95},
		{"id":"d90922cb957b8ec6","type":"text","text":"#### GPe","x":4721,"y":-806,"width":88,"height":60},
		{"id":"fc05ee021741d387","type":"text","text":"D2 Reecptors","x":5020,"y":-1172,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"057a98ffd19f990a","type":"text","text":"![[image-31.png]]","x":5280,"y":-1437,"width":860,"height":707},
		{"id":"1631afd485d0f6f5","type":"text","text":"![[image-35.png]]\n\nThis fig. shows how a person with a lesion in the\nright posterior parietal cortex might try to copy drawings.\n\nIn these cases, the patient’s ability to copy the left side of\nthe drawings is severely impaired. Also, such a person will\nalways try to avoid using his or her left arm, left hand, or\nother portions of his or her left body for the performance\nof tasks; the person may not even wash this side of the\nbody (personal neglect syndrome), almost not knowing\nthat these parts of the body exist.","x":5765,"y":370,"width":540,"height":890},
		{"id":"258c785bb42169fa","type":"text","text":"Caudate Nucleus","x":-1500,"y":-2794,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7ce5420ed7cb47bf","type":"text","text":"SNc","x":4480,"y":-1417,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"13d6f31be285afff","type":"text","text":"# D1 Medium Spiny Neuron","x":4336,"y":-1117,"width":250,"height":95},
		{"id":"1f3b81edeffcf58d","type":"text","text":"#### GPi / SNr","x":4401,"y":-806,"width":120,"height":60}
